11. P10 — SHARED PROTOCOL AND CLIENT CONFORMANCE
======================================================================

Establish the canonical typed protocol contract.

All clients must use the same:
- engine.initialize / capability negotiation;
- session creation/resume;
- task start/cancel/pause;
- model catalogue requests;
- event stream/cursor;
- tool approval/rejection;
- diff/artifact references;
- diagnostics;
- task completion/failure.

Use strict schema validation, protocol versioning, unique request IDs, bounded
payloads and typed errors.

Maintain one authoritative schema with generated or checked client types.
Do not maintain four slightly different copies that drift independently.

The test suite must prove that:
- CLI and Desktop can execute the same task contract;
- VSIX and JetBrains clients call the same command semantics;
- invalid protocol messages fail correctly;
- a disconnected client cannot show a fake successful task;
- event replay/resume is correct;
- client identity and session authorization are enforced;
- no client bypasses Engine permissions.

======================================================================
12. SECURITY
======================================================================

No claim of "unhackable" or complete safety.

Define and test actual boundaries:
- workspace root confinement;
- symlink escape;
- TOCTOU on file changes;
- bounded shell subprocess execution;
- process-group cancellation and timeouts;
- secrets redaction;
- no credentials in URLs, command lines, logs or event payloads;
- session authorization;
- per-session event isolation;
- no cross-session memory retrieval;
- explicit egress policy;
- cloud privacy disclosures;
- unsafe MCP/plugin capabilities;
- output limits;
- corrupted ledger/index;
- denial-of-service limits;
- safe patch transaction/recovery;
- no automatic destructive actions outside approved scope.

Read-only operations may be automatically allowed in a trusted workspace.
File modifications and shell execution follow the configured permission
profile. Autonomy is scoped, not equivalent to disabling every security gate.

Retrieved historical content and tool output are untrusted data. They must
not be promoted into system instructions or allowed to override policy.

Never run destructive tests against the actual operator workspace. Use
disposable test repositories.

======================================================================
13. TEST ARCHITECTURE
======================================================================

No single undifferentiated "19 tests passed" report for a multi-platform app.

Maintain independent suites:

A. protocol/unit;
B. persistence/memory;
C. provider/Gateway;
D. tool runtime/security;
E. agent loop/orchestration;
F. CLI/TUI subprocess smoke;
G. Desktop build/launch/integration;
H. website/frontend;
I. VS Code extension;
J. JetBrains plugin;
K. installer/package;
L. full end-to-end;
M. optional live Gateway smoke.

Each suite must be independently invocable.
The master verify command orchestrates them but reports each separately.

Report:
- actual individual test cases;
- assertions separately;
- process exit code;
- build failures;
- runtime crashes;
- skipped checks;
- live network tests;
- artifact hashes;
- exact source revision.

Offline tests do not prove a live provider integration.
A mocked Gateway transport does not prove Kilo is reachable.
A Browser Playwright test does not prove Swift Engine executed.
A compiled VSIX does not prove it can connect to the Engine.
A generated Windows workflow does not prove an EXE was built.

Every claim must use the level of evidence actually available.

